'''Cagaarka Tower''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''Daarta Cagaarka'', "Green Place Tower") is a 22-storey [[International Style (architecture)|International Style]] office building at 93 [[Dhib Street]] in [[District 3, Samara|District 3]], [[Samara]], [[Burawa]]. Completed in 1964, it is named for the [[Cagaarka]] — the historic green quarter of the city, known for its parks and public gardens. The tower's two primary tenants are the [[Burawi Fotbal Listing Exchange]] (BFLE), which has been headquartered in the building since 1997, and the regional offices of the [[Burawi Fotbal Federation]] (BFF).

== Location ==

Cagaarka Tower is located in [[District 3, Samara|District 3]], one of Samara's central administrative districts. District 3 borders [[Kankadadka]], the capital of the [[Kankadadka Capital Region]], and its proximity to the regional capital has made it a natural location for regional government offices and institutional headquarters. The district houses a concentration of regional administrative offices, government agencies, and public-sector organisations that serve the broader Kankadadka-Samara metropolitan area.

The building's address at 93 Dhib Street places it on one of District 3's principal commercial streets, within walking distance of the Samara Metro and the regional rail interchange.

== History ==

Cagaarka Tower was completed in 1964, during the period of post-[[Great Adonian War|war]] reconstruction and modernisation that reshaped Samara's central districts. The building was designed in the [[International Style (architecture)|International Style]], characterised by its rectilinear form, reinforced concrete frame, ribbon windows, and absence of applied ornament — a style that dominated Burawi public architecture of the 1960s and reflected the socialist government's emphasis on functional, egalitarian design.

The building was originally constructed to house regional government offices for the [[Kankadadka Capital Region]]. Its 22 storeys made it one of the tallest buildings in Samara at the time of its completion, and its location in District 3 — adjacent to the regional capital — was chosen to facilitate coordination between Samara-based regional agencies and the Kankadadka government.

=== BFF and BFLE tenancy ===

The [[Burawi Fotbal Federation]] (BFF) established its regional offices in Cagaarka Tower in 1989, relocating from smaller premises in central Samara. The BFF's presence in the building was a factor in the [[Burawi Fotbal Listing Exchange|BFLE]]'s decision to move to the tower in 1997, as the exchange's regulatory relationship with the BFF made proximity to the federation's regional offices advantageous.

The BFLE occupies floors 14 through 18, including the exchange's trading floor, compliance and regulatory offices, and the BFLE's administrative headquarters. The BFF's regional offices occupy floors 19 through 21, handling regional league administration, academy coordination, and draft logistics for the Kankadadka Capital Region. The shared building has facilitated close operational coordination between the two organisations, particularly during the annual draft period and the promotion/relegation season.

== Architecture ==

Cagaarka Tower is a reinforced concrete structure with a rectilinear plan and a curtain wall facade of tinted glass and precast concrete panels. The building's International Style design is typical of Burawi commercial architecture of the 1960s: horizontal emphasis, flat roofline, and minimal ornamentation. The facade is divided into regular bays by vertical concrete mullions, with ribbon windows on each floor providing uniform natural light to the office interiors.

The ground floor and mezzanine contain a public lobby, retail units, and the building's main entrance on Dhib Street. The BFLE's stadium kiosk — a physical share-trading station that operates on match days — is located in the lobby, providing walk-in trading access for supporters visiting the building.

